Practical NO: 06

Basic Shell Commands and Redirect: Use essential shell commands and output redirection.

Theory: Shell commands are used to perform basic operations in Linux. Redirection (>, >>) sends output to a file, while a pipe (|) sends the output of one command as input to another command.

Requirements:

•Linux / Ubuntu Terminal
•Basic knowledge of shell commands
•Commands: echo, ls, sort, wc, cat.

a) Display text and variables using echo.

$ echo "hello SYCS batch 1 students" : Displays the given text on the terminal.

$ name="Tushar" : Stores Tushar in the variable name.

$ echo "hello $name" : Displays the value of the variable name.

b) Use redirection operators > and >>.

$ echo "training" > textfile.txt : Writes text into the file and overwrites old content.

$ echo "testing" >> textfile.txt : Adds text to the end of the file.

$ cat textfile.txt : Displays the contents of the file.

c) Use pipes (|) with commands like ls | sort.

$ ls | sort : Displays files and directories in alphabetical order.

d) Count lines / words using wc

$ ls | wc -l : Counts the number of lines / items in the output of ls.

$ who | wc -l : Counts the number of currently logged-in users.
